{
  "question": "For OUD with chronic pain, can buprenorphine continue long term, can doses be split, and what monitoring is needed?",
  "status": "answered",
  "assumption": "Assuming VA clinical guidance (clinical question, no program named).",
  "answer": "Assuming VA guidance:\n\n- **Long term:** The VA/DoD 2022 CPG doesn't set a stop date. It suggests buprenorphine over full agonists for daily opioids (weak for). Bup/naloxone may be the preferred opioid for comorbid pain and OUD. The CPG found insufficient evidence to favor methadone, buprenorphine or XR-naltrexone for OUD with chronic pain.\n- **Splitting:** Yes. For patients on bup or bup/naloxone for OUD, the 24-hr dose can be split BID or TID for pain. Doses up to 16–24 mg/day in divided doses are allowed if needed.\n- **Monitoring (VA PBM, 2021):** Check at baseline and at least annually. Check more often at initiation or with unexpected results. Items:\n  - PEG pain score\n  - PDMP\n  - UDS\n  - Renal/hepatic function\n  - Suicide screen\n  - Naloxone and overdose education\n  - ECG if at QTc risk\n\nPain and OUD prescribing requires a DEA-X waivered clinician, and the Rx should say \"for pain management.\"",
